// bubbler profile: code
//
// This profile shares ~/Projects, so `mkdir ~/Projects` — or edit the
// `home-share` node to the tree you keep code in — before the first run:
// a share whose source is missing is an error, not a quieter sandbox.
//
// `--wait` is part of the command, not a preference: the `code` launcher
// starts the editor detached and returns about a second later, and a
// sandbox ends when its command does — without it the window maps and
// dies with the run that made it (measured). `--wait` returns when the
// window closes, which is when this instance should stop.
//
// `dri` is not optional here. Without it every VS Code process still
// starts — zygote, renderer, gpu-process on the Wayland ozone backend —
// and no window ever maps (measured).
//
// `dbus` and `portals` are the desktop's side: the editor reads the
// colour scheme over org.freedesktop.portal.Settings as it starts
// (measured), and its file chooser is then the portal's, which reaches
// outside the shared directories and exports what you pick into this
// instance's document view. Without them the chooser is the one inside
// the sandbox and sees the private home and ~/Projects.
//
// VS Code keeps its own multi-process sandbox inside bubbler's. Arch's
// /usr/share/code/chrome-sandbox is NOT setuid, unlike
// /usr/lib/electron*/chrome-sandbox, so Electron takes the user-namespace
// path directly — which means `userns "disable"` would break it and must
// never be added here.
//
// No `env ELECTRON_OZONE_PLATFORM_HINT`: Electron 42 (VS Code 1.133)
// selects the Wayland ozone platform on its own, measured with the hint
// absent. Extra flags go in the private home's code-flags.conf, which
// /usr/bin/code reads from $XDG_CONFIG_HOME.
//
// This is a code editor, not a build box. Nothing here reaches the host:
// no host filesystem, no ssh-agent socket, and no bus name that runs a
// command outside the sandbox — `org.freedesktop.Flatpak` is one such
// name, and granting it would make the sandbox a shell on the host. A
// remote or container extension will not work, and git over SSH needs a
// key in the private home rather than your agent.
//
// A checkout outside the home is named explicitly, e.g.
//     path-share "/kioxia/src" mode=rw
//
// `network` is the sandbox's own network namespace, so a dev server on
// the host's own 127.0.0.1 is not reachable from the editor, and one the
// editor starts is not reachable from a host browser. Add
// `network { allow-port 3000 }` to publish a port of the sandbox on the
// host's loopback — the server inside must listen on 0.0.0.0, not on its
// own loopback — or write `network "host"` to share the host's stack.
//
// Bare by design. Add:
//   dbus {
//       talk "org.freedesktop.secrets"
//   }
//   lint-allow {
//       "secrets-access" reason="extensions keep their tokens in the Secret Storage API, which is this bus name"
//       "dbus-name-is-risky" reason="the Secret Storage API extensions keep their tokens in is this bus name"
//   }
//     — the block goes in place of the bare `dbus` node above, since one
//       config carries one `dbus` node. The name is the Secret Storage
//       API that extensions keep their tokens in; without it, one that
//       signs in asks again every start. It has no per-application
//       partitioning, so the rule also lets VS Code read every other
//       secret in the login keyring.
//   notify
//     — desktop notifications, the ones an extension or an update raises.
//       Needs `dbus`.
wayland
dri
network
dbus
portals
home-share "Projects" mode=rw
command "code" "--wait"
